Continuous diffusion language models retain partially resolved token information across denoising steps. We study whether pretrained autoregressive (AR) LLMs can acquire this generation mechanism with limited additional data and training.
We present \textbf{Semi-Continuous Diffusion Language Model (SC-dLLM)}, the first low-data conversion of pretrained AR LLMs to blockwise continuous-state generation. Each block starts from masks, evolves through continuous states, and yields discrete tokens. A brief continuous endpoint initialization prepares the backbone for same-position prediction. The main adaptation then trains on corrupted intermediate states while retaining the vocabulary prediction objective. During generation, token predictions guide stochastic updates and determine when confident positions are committed. Training uses analytically sampled states. The 7B conversion uses approximately 1.1B additional training tokens.
Across code generation, mathematics, instruction following, and knowledge-intensive QA, SC-dLLM reaches \revauthor{average scores} of \revreview{46.8} at 1.5B and \revreview{63.89} at 7B, exceeding the AR-initializer, \revreview{AR fine-tuning,} Fast-dLLM v2\revreview{, and matched Fast-dLLM v2-FT} averages at both scales. At 7B, all four HumanEval and MBPP Base/Plus scores exceed both the same-data AR fine-tuning control and Fast-dLLM v2. \revreview{At a commitment threshold of 0.9, the 7B model also exceeds Fast-dLLM v2 on HumanEval Plus and MBPP Plus with 1.27$\times$ and 1.14$\times$ throughput, respectively.}
